WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:07.000
In this session we will see how the electric motors are used in the automotive field.

00:07.000 --> 00:13.000
We will see particular configuration and examples of car.

00:18.000 --> 00:24.000
Just a reminder to the classification of hybrid vehicle, we can have

00:24.000 --> 00:32.000
Milled hybrid electric vehicle, also known as MHEV.

00:32.000 --> 00:39.000
The vehicle is driven by a combustion engine powered by a compact electric motor.

00:39.000 --> 00:44.000
It can have the electric motor in different positions.

00:44.000 --> 00:51.000
Full hybrid electric vehicle, also known as FHEV.

00:51.000 --> 00:56.000
It has both combustion and electric engines.

00:56.000 --> 01:02.000
At low speed and short distances, a fully electric propulsion is possible.

01:02.000 --> 01:08.000
The engine hybrid electric vehicle, also known as PHEV.

01:08.000 --> 01:14.000
It reaches higher speeds and longer distances, unlike full hybrid.

01:14.000 --> 01:26.000
They can only be charged from external points and can have a larger battery pack than the FHEV.

01:26.000 --> 01:32.000
Because the size is not limited to the stored kinetic energy.

01:32.000 --> 01:40.000
On the right of this slide, you can see all the position of the engines in an hybrid vehicle's architecture.

01:40.000 --> 01:49.000
Hybrid vehicles uses an internal combustion engine and at least one electric motor.

01:49.000 --> 01:59.000
In hybrid electric vehicles, we can find many abbreviations as P0, P1, P2, P3, P4 and P5.

01:59.000 --> 02:05.000
Each abbreviation depends on the position of the electric motor in the hybrid powertrain.

02:05.000 --> 02:18.000
In some way, the number identifies the distance between the electric motor and the wheels, which decreases from P0 to P5.

02:18.000 --> 02:28.000
In the P0 configuration, the electric motor can be mounted in place of the alternator and then connected to the engine with a belt.

02:28.000 --> 02:35.000
These are belt driven starter generators, which also act as a starter motor and generator.

02:35.000 --> 02:42.000
In the P1 configuration, the electric motor is inserted on the motor shaft.

02:42.000 --> 02:49.000
It runs at the same speed as the crankshaft and requires some more modifications.

02:49.000 --> 02:57.000
In this slide, we can see an example of P0 configuration in Audi system.

02:57.000 --> 03:08.000
This car is the Audi A8 48 voltage M HEP.

03:08.000 --> 03:14.000
We can see a DC DC converter to pass from 12 to 48 voltages.

03:14.000 --> 03:21.000
We can see a 12 voltage battery and the 48 volt battery.

03:21.000 --> 03:31.000
The battery is needed for the integrated BSG, a motor in P0 configuration right here.

03:31.000 --> 03:36.000
And this is the internal combustion engine.

03:36.000 --> 03:46.000
In P2 configuration, the electric motor is placed between the engine and the transmission, directly on the shaft or through a gearbox.

03:46.000 --> 03:54.000
The engine insertion system implies a clutch that disconnects the internal combustion engine.

03:54.000 --> 04:02.000
So it is possible to drive in full electric by the opening of the clutch.

04:02.000 --> 04:06.000
We can have two configurations to put the P2 motor.

04:06.000 --> 04:14.000
We can have the integrated electric motor configuration or the side electric motor configuration.

04:14.000 --> 04:20.000
In this slide, we can see an example of P2 configuration in Porsche Panamera system.

04:20.000 --> 04:33.000
We can see the internal combustion engine, the electric motor in P2 configuration, the ZF transmission and the battery pack in the posterior part of the car.

04:33.000 --> 04:39.000
Here we can see the P2.5 configuration.

04:39.000 --> 04:44.000
This configuration is possible only with dual clutch transmission.

04:44.000 --> 04:52.000
The electric motor is placed inside the transmission on one of the two concentric transmission shafts.

04:52.000 --> 05:05.000
So that one shaft is completely driven by the internal combustion engine while the other one is driven also by electric machine.

05:05.000 --> 05:18.000
Electric creeping or drive is possible by moving just even gear shafts, but also combined combustion and electric power mode is possible.

05:18.000 --> 05:28.000
In P3 configuration, this configuration moves the engine downstream of the gearbox.

05:28.000 --> 05:37.000
In this way, losses are minimized since the electric motor only drives the final part of transmission.

05:37.000 --> 05:47.000
In the P4 configuration, the electric motor acts exclusively on the axis not connected to the internal combustion engine.

05:47.000 --> 05:55.000
In this way, it's possible to have an all-wheel drive without mechanical connection with the thermal engine.

05:55.000 --> 06:02.000
Even the pure electric mode is easily obtainable by disconnecting the main transmission.

06:02.000 --> 06:10.000
In this slide, we can see an example of P4 configuration in Toyota Prius system.

06:10.000 --> 06:17.000
We can find the internal combustion engine, the battery pack, one induction electric motor and the inverter.

06:17.000 --> 06:23.000
In this slide, we can find the P5 configuration.

06:23.000 --> 06:35.000
For hybrid and full electric vehicle, there is the Cronimus P5, which indicates the motors in the wheels, a solution that eliminates the distance.

06:35.000 --> 06:44.000
This configuration, unlike P0, P1, P2, P3 and P4, has a still limited application in production cars.

06:44.000 --> 07:00.000
P2 to P4 configuration, as I said, have the electric motors placed after the clutch, connected to the driveline side, and that can improve overall electrical performances.

07:00.000 --> 07:13.000
Another electric motor, a P1, can be added to the engine side, enlarging hybrid strategies in terms of load shift, torque assist, regeneration, etc.

07:13.000 --> 07:26.000
On the other hand, complexity and costs increase exponentially due to the addiction of another motor that should work together all the other components.

07:26.000 --> 07:34.000
This configuration essentially combines both benefits and disadvantages of P1, P2 and P4.

07:34.000 --> 07:40.000
Battery electric vehicle, also known as BEV.

07:40.000 --> 07:48.000
The power train of an electric vehicle powered by battery is the simplest one among all vehicles.

07:48.000 --> 07:59.000
It must include an onboard battery charger, OBC, and there are several solutions for its configuration.

07:59.000 --> 08:09.000
Here we can see a fuel cell electric vehicle, also known as FCEV.

08:09.000 --> 08:15.000
The electrical power train is the same as for the BEV.

08:15.000 --> 08:21.000
The difference comes from the energy source that in this case is the fuel cell.

08:21.000 --> 08:26.000
We can see in the picture the system used by Toyota Mirai.

08:26.000 --> 08:38.000
In this slide you can find the pros and cons of the P0 configuration.

08:38.000 --> 08:59.000
In this slide you can find a list of pros and cons of P1 configuration.

08:59.000 --> 09:17.000
In this slide you can find a list of pros and cons of the P2 configuration and P5 configuration.

09:29.000 --> 09:39.000
In this slide we can find the pros and cons of induction motor.

09:39.000 --> 09:43.000
The advantages of induction motor we have railability.

09:43.000 --> 09:54.000
Induction motors are robust and railable with low maintenance requirements, making them a good choice for applications that require long life.

09:54.000 --> 10:06.000
We have low cost because induction motors are widely available and relatively inexpensive compared to other types of electric motors.

10:06.000 --> 10:10.000
Like efficiency at low to medium speeds.

10:10.000 --> 10:28.000
The disadvantages we have efficiency because induction motors are less efficient than PMSM motors due to the need for separate excitation sources and the losses associated with them.

10:28.000 --> 10:46.000
We have torque speed curves present, an induction high speed region which begins at the motor critical speed beyond which if the motor is operated at maximum current the motor will stall.

10:46.000 --> 11:03.000
Tesla made a significant change to its electric motor strategy with the introduction of the Model 3, switching from an AC induction motor to a permanent magnet motor.

11:03.000 --> 11:11.000
Here we can find the pros and cons about PMSM motor.

11:11.000 --> 11:20.000
In pros we can find the high power to weight ratio which makes them a good choice for applications where weight is a concern.

11:20.000 --> 11:23.000
BLDC is better than PMSM.

11:23.000 --> 11:39.000
We have high efficiency because PM motors have high efficiency because of their design which use permanent magnet to produce a magnetic field and eliminate the need for a separate excitation source.

11:39.000 --> 11:44.000
In this PMSM is better than BLDC.

11:44.000 --> 12:01.000
Responsiveness because PM motors are highly responsive allowing precise control of speed and torque making them well suited for applications that require precise control.

12:01.000 --> 12:11.000
In the cons we have high costs due to permanent magnet and the need for complex control electronics.

12:11.000 --> 12:16.000
This makes them less accessible for some applications.

12:16.000 --> 12:25.000
We can find a list of pros and cons of SWOXSetrack-reluctance model.

12:25.000 --> 12:36.000
All of these slices of theCK sensitize

